/* THE CONFESSION - Danny talks to the raccoon */
/* This is the MIDPOINT - the emotional heart of the film */
/* Approximate page count: 2-2.5 pages */
/* Power arc: FALSE CONTROL -> EXPOSED */

INT. DANNY'S TRUCK (MOVING) - NIGHT

The road unspools ahead. Empty. 2 AM quiet.

Streetlights strobe through the windshield at regular
intervals. Light. Dark. Light. Dark. Each one paints Danny's
face in orange, then shadow.

His hands grip the wheel at ten and two. Textbook. The grip
of a man proving he is in control.

The IID handset hangs from the steering column. Green light
glowing. Victory.

In the passenger seat: the RACCOON. Seatbelt stretched across
its belly. Eyes closed. One paw curled against its chest like
a sleeping child.

Its chest rises. Falls. Rises. Falls.

Danny glances at it. Then back at the road.

Then at it again.

DANNY
(quiet, almost to himself)
Holy shit.

He laughs. Short. Surprised. The sound of a man who cannot
believe what he just did.

DANNY (CONT'D)
It actually worked.

He shakes his head. Looks at the raccoon again.

DANNY (CONT'D)
Hey. Hey, buddy. You did good.

The raccoon does not respond. Its chest rises. Falls.

DANNY (CONT'D)
Real good. You're a hero. Gonna get
you back to your... dumpster, or
wherever. Soon as I get home. Thirty
minutes, tops.

Silence. The engine hums. The streetlights strobe.

Danny's thumbs tap the steering wheel. Nervous energy looking
for an exit.

DANNY (CONT'D)
Rachel would've loved this.

A beat. He wasn't expecting that to come out.

DANNY (CONT'D)
My wife. Ex-wife. Soon to be. Still
technically... it's complicated.

He glances at the raccoon like it might judge him.

It doesn't. It can't. That's the point.

DANNY (CONT'D)
She left four months ago. Probably
should've left earlier. I would've
left earlier if I was her.

The truck passes under a streetlight. Orange wash.
Danny's face in sharp relief.

DANNY (CONT'D)
You know what's funny? I didn't even
fight it. She said she was leaving
and I said okay. Just... okay.
Didn't yell. Didn't beg. Didn't do
any of the things you're supposed
to do.

He rubs his thumb and forefinger together. The welder's tic.

DANNY (CONT'D)
I just watched her pack.

Another streetlight. Dark. Light. Dark.

DANNY (CONT'D)
She wrote me a letter. Sent it
three weeks later. I've read it maybe
twenty times. Can't finish it. Every
time I get to the part where she
says... where she says she was
scared of me...

His voice catches. He clears his throat.

DANNY (CONT'D)
I punched a door. Once. Came home
from the bar, couldn't find my keys,
and I just... punched the door frame.
Broke two knuckles. She was inside.
She heard.

He looks at his right hand on the wheel. The knuckles
still slightly swollen.

DANNY (CONT'D)
She wasn't scared I'd hurt her. She
knew I wouldn't. She was scared of
what I was becoming. That's worse,
you know? That's so much worse.

The raccoon's paw twitches. Just reflexive. Danny
doesn't notice.

DANNY (CONT'D)
I have these... attacks. Since 2012.
Heart racing, can't breathe, feel
like I'm dying. Rachel was the only
one who knew. She'd hold my hand and
tell me to count backwards from a
hundred. By the time I got to sixty
it was usually over.

He swallows.

DANNY (CONT'D)
I haven't had anyone to count with
in four months.

A long pause. The road stretches ahead. Endless.

DANNY (CONT'D)
My sponsor - I got a sponsor, Mike,
I did the whole thing - his number's
in my phone. "DO NOT ANSWER AFTER
2100." That's what he told me to
save it as. So I'd call during the
day. When I could think straight.

He laughs. Bitter.

DANNY (CONT'D)
Scrolled past it three times tonight.
Three times. Just... kept going.
Because calling would mean admitting
I couldn't handle it.

He glances at the raccoon. Its face peaceful. Unknowing.

DANNY (CONT'D)
And I can always handle it. Right?
That's my whole thing. Danny Vega,
the guy who figures it out. The guy
who doesn't need anyone.

His jaw tightens.

DANNY (CONT'D)
My dad - he broke down once, on the
99. Hundred-two degrees. Triple-A
couldn't get there for hours. So he
fixed it himself. Jury-rigged the
fuel pump with a coat hanger. Got
us home. Never said a word about it.

Beat.

DANNY (CONT'D)
I've been trying to be that guy my
whole life. Solve your own problems.
Don't wait for help.

His voice drops.

DANNY (CONT'D)
Help isn't coming.

Silence. Just the engine. The road.

Danny reaches over. Hesitates. Then gently touches
the raccoon's fur. Just once. Just to feel something
that isn't steering wheel.

DANNY (CONT'D)
(almost a whisper)
You're the first thing I've talked
to in months that doesn't expect
anything from me.

He pulls his hand back.

DANNY (CONT'D)
You just sit there. You just...
exist. You don't need me to be
okay. You don't need me to have
a plan. You don't need me to be
the guy who figures it out.

His voice cracks.

DANNY (CONT'D)
I'm so tired of being that guy.

He wipes his eyes with the back of his hand. Quick.
Embarrassed even though no one is watching.

The streetlights keep strobing. Light. Dark. Light. Dark.

DANNY (CONT'D)
You know what I miss? I miss being
small. I miss being twelve years old
and not knowing how to fix anything
and that being okay. That being
allowed.

He stares at the road.

DANNY (CONT'D)
Now everything's my fault. If it
breaks, I should've prevented it.
If it fails, I should've seen it
coming. If she leaves...

He can't finish.

The raccoon's paw twitches again. Slightly longer this time.
Danny still doesn't notice.

DANNY (CONT'D)
I'm telling all this to a raccoon.

He laughs. Not funny. Real.

DANNY (CONT'D)
An unconscious raccoon that I found
in a dumpster. That I strapped into
my passenger seat. That I used to
beat a breathalyzer so I could drive
drunk for the second time in six
months.

Beat.

DANNY (CONT'D)
That's who I am. That's the guy
Rachel left. That's the guy who
couldn't make Chief. That's the
guy who punches doors.

He grips the wheel tighter.

DANNY (CONT'D)
And I still can't call Mike. Even
now. Even after all of it. I still
can't pick up the phone and say--

His voice breaks.

DANNY (CONT'D)
I need help.

The words hang in the cab. First time he has ever said
them out loud. To anyone. Even a raccoon.

The streetlights strobe.

Danny wipes his face again. Sets his jaw. Stares ahead.

DANNY (CONT'D)
(quieter now)
It's fine.

But it sounds different now. Hollow. Even he doesn't
believe it anymore.

DANNY (CONT'D)
This is fine.

CLOSE ON: The raccoon's face.

Peaceful. Still.

Then--

The tiniest flicker behind closed eyelids.

HOLD ON this image. The calm before.

Danny doesn't see it. He's watching the road.

DANNY (CONT'D)
(almost to himself)
Almost home.

The raccoon's ear twitches.

FADE TO BLACK.

/* End scene - approximately 2.5 pages */
/* Note: The wood chipper moment is the touch - Danny reaching */
/* over to feel the raccoon's fur. That's when we break. */
/* The confession earns the betrayal that comes next. */
